* stgcolor.scheme
* dash translation of Stata's stgcolor scheme.
*
* PROVENANCE
*   Source Stata scheme file : /Applications/StataNow/ado/base/s/scheme-stgcolor.scheme
*   Stata #include chain     : s2color -> stcolor -> stgcolor
*   Stata version            : StataNow 19.5 (MP), born 12 Aug 2026
*   Stata style dir          : /Applications/StataNow/ado/base/style
*   Derived from             : test/fixtures/scheme_truth.json entry "stgcolor",
*                              generated by scripts/extract_scheme_truth.py
*   Fixture generated (UTC)  : 2026-09-19T16:13:55Z
*   This file written        : 2026-09-19
*
*   Regenerate the fixture with scripts/extract_scheme_truth.py and re-check this
*   file against it after any Stata upgrade; the values below were true of the
*   Stata version named above and of no other.
*
* WHY HEX AND NOT STATA COLOUR NAMES
*   Every colour below is written as a resolved #rrggbb value taken from the
*   fixture, which resolved it through Stata's own style/color-*.style files.
*   OptionTranslator._COLOR_MAP carries only 72 names and does not know stc1..
*   stc15, and SchemeParser._color() swallows an unresolvable token and returns
*   its "#000000" default -- which is what made the stcolor family render every
*   series black.  Hex is self-contained and cannot be swallowed.
*
* PROPERTY CATEGORIES USED BY dash SchemeParser:
*   color, symbol, linepattern, linewidth, intensity, gsize, graphsize,
*   symbolsize, clockdir, numstyle
*
* OMISSIONS (project rule 2 -- honesty)
*   None.  The fixture records no null and an empty "unresolvable" list for
*   stgcolor, so every property below carries a value Stata actually resolved.
*   Two directives are recorded that SchemeParser._init_properties() does not yet
*   expose as attributes -- "yesno draw_major_hgrid" and "yesno draw_major_grid".
*   They are not invented dash keys: they are the exact directive names the
*   fixture's own sources map records, they parse into SchemeParser._props, and
*   test_scheme_fidelity.py checks for them by those names.  The attributes that
*   consume them land in section 11.

label "Stata graph color (stgcolor)"

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Background and region colors
* fixture: background_color, plotregion_color, legend_bg_color,
*          legend_border_color, grid_color, minor_grid_color, title_color,
*          axis_line_color
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
color background      #ffffff
color plotregion      #ffffff
color legend          #ffffff
color legend_line     #ffffff
color major_grid      #f0f0f0
color minor_grid      #505050
color heading         #000000
color axisline        #000000

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Per-plot color cycle (p1-p15)
* fixture: p_colors; Stata source directives "color p1".."color p15"
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
color p1      #1a85ff
color p2      #d41159
color p3      #00bf7f
color p4      #ffd400
color p5      #4f2c99
color p6      #ff6333
color p7      #4db7ff
color p8      #7c0015
color p9      #0fefaf
color p10     #faa307
color p11     #758bfd
color p12     #fed9b7
color p13     #08234c
color p14     #f88dad
color p15     #0f5156

* Per-plot line color (line/connected plots).
* Stata declares no distinct "color p#line" for stgcolor: the fixture's sources
* map records these resolving through the p#stub -> p# fallback to "color p#".
* Written out explicitly so dash does not depend on its own fallback.
color p1line   #1a85ff
color p2line   #d41159
color p3line   #00bf7f
color p4line   #ffd400
color p5line   #4f2c99
color p6line   #ff6333
color p7line   #4db7ff
color p8line   #7c0015
color p9line   #0fefaf
color p10line  #faa307
color p11line  #758bfd
color p12line  #fed9b7
color p13line  #08234c
color p14line  #f88dad
color p15line  #0f5156

* Per-plot bar/area fill color.  Same fallback as p#line: source is "color p#".
color p1bar    #1a85ff
color p2bar    #d41159
color p3bar    #00bf7f
color p4bar    #ffd400
color p5bar    #4f2c99
color p6bar    #ff6333
color p7bar    #4db7ff
color p8bar    #7c0015
color p9bar    #0fefaf
color p10bar   #faa307
color p11bar   #758bfd
color p12bar   #fed9b7
color p13bar   #08234c
color p14bar   #f88dad
color p15bar   #0f5156

* Per-plot marker fill color (scatter).  Same fallback: source is "color p#".
color p1mark   #1a85ff
color p2mark   #d41159
color p3mark   #00bf7f
color p4mark   #ffd400
color p5mark   #4f2c99
color p6mark   #ff6333
color p7mark   #4db7ff
color p8mark   #7c0015
color p9mark   #0fefaf
color p10mark  #faa307
color p11mark  #758bfd
color p12mark  #fed9b7
color p13mark  #08234c
color p14mark  #f88dad
color p15mark  #0f5156

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Marker symbol cycle
* Stata stgcolor sets ONE class-level directive, "symbol p circle", so all 15
* plots share the same marker.  dash keys symbols per plot, so the single Stata
* value is written fifteen times -- this is a faithful expansion, not variety
* that Stata does not have.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
symbol p1     circle
symbol p2     circle
symbol p3     circle
symbol p4     circle
symbol p5     circle
symbol p6     circle
symbol p7     circle
symbol p8     circle
symbol p9     circle
symbol p10    circle
symbol p11    circle
symbol p12    circle
symbol p13    circle
symbol p14    circle
symbol p15    circle

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Line patterns
* As with symbols, Stata sets one class-level "linepattern p solid"; every plot
* in stgcolor draws a solid line.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
linepattern p1line   solid
linepattern p2line   solid
linepattern p3line   solid
linepattern p4line   solid
linepattern p5line   solid
linepattern p6line   solid
linepattern p7line   solid
linepattern p8line   solid
linepattern p9line   solid
linepattern p10line  solid
linepattern p11line  solid
linepattern p12line  solid
linepattern p13line  solid
linepattern p14line  solid
linepattern p15line  solid

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Line widths (Stata style names; "medium" is .30 in Stata linewidth units)
* fixture: default_line_width, grid_line_width
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
linewidth p           medium
linewidth major_grid  medium

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Fill intensities
* fixture: fill_alpha (intensity p = inten80), bar_alpha (intensity bar = full),
*          area_alpha = full.  No Stata base scheme sets "intensity area"; the
*          fixture records it resolving through the bare "intensity" class
*          default.  dash reads only the "intensity area" key, so the resolved
*          class-default value is written under that key.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
intensity p        inten80
intensity bar      full
intensity area     full

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Font sizes (Stata gsize keywords)
* fixture: text_size, heading_size, axis_title_size, tick_label_size, note_size
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
gsize text          medium
gsize heading       large
gsize axis_title    medsmall
gsize tick_label    medsmall
gsize note          small

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Graph dimensions, in inches (consumed by section 10 for figure proportions)
* fixture: graphsize_x, graphsize_y
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
graphsize x  3.987
graphsize y  2.392

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Default marker size for the p cycle (Stata style name)
* fixture: symbolsize (small = 1.04166 relative units)
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
symbolsize p  small

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Legend placement.  Not in the task's property list, but both keys are read by
* SchemeParser._init_properties() and both are present in the fixture, so they
* are recorded rather than dropped.
* fixture: legend_position (clock direction 3), legend_cols
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
clockdir legend_position  3
numstyle legend_cols      1

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Grid switches.  Stata stgcolor draws horizontal major grid lines only.
* fixture: draw_major_hgrid ("yesno draw_major_hgrid"),
*          draw_major_vgrid ("yesno draw_major_grid" -- note the directive name
*          carries no "v"; that is Stata's spelling, not a typo).
* Parsed into SchemeParser._props today; no attribute consumes them until
* section 11.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* GRID VISIBILITY.  A Stata grid belongs to an AXIS and the key is named for
* that axis's orientation, not for the direction of the lines:
*   style/axisstyle-horizontal_default.style:18  draw_major_grid <- draw_major_hgrid
*   style/axisstyle-vertical_default.style:18    draw_major_grid <- draw_major_vgrid
* The horizontal axis is x, and its major grid lines are the VERTICAL ones at
* the x ticks; the vertical axis is y, and its lines are HORIZONTAL.
* `yesno draw_major_grid' below is transcribed faithfully from Stata's file but
* is DEAD at scheme level: grep "scheme draw_major_grid" over
* /Applications/StataNow/ado/base/style/ returns nothing.  dash used to read it
* for the y axis, which is why every scheme reported "no grid" and dash drew
* both anyway.
yesno draw_major_hgrid  yes
yesno draw_major_vgrid yes
yesno draw_major_grid   no

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Added 22sep2026 by scripts/extract_scheme_truth.py (WP-16): directives
* Stata consumes that the translation above did not carry.  Values are
* resolved from the same Stata #include chain and style files as the
* rest of this file (chain: s2color -> stcolor -> stgcolor); colours are
* pre-resolved #rrggbb.  Re-running the script re-checks every directive
* in this file and appends only what is missing.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

* Minor grid switches -- EFFECTIVE per-axis values.  twoway builds the x
* axis with `axisstyle horizontal_default' and the y axis with `axisstyle
* vertical_default'; the scheme maps each role to a style name, and that
* style file names the `yesno' key Stata actually reads:
*   x axis: axisstyle horizontal_default = horizontal_default
*           -> style/axisstyle-horizontal_default.style
*           -> draw_major_hgrid / draw_minor_hgrid
*   y axis: axisstyle vertical_default = vertical_default
*           -> style/axisstyle-vertical_default.style
*           -> draw_major_vgrid / draw_minor_vgrid
* The draw_major_hgrid / draw_major_vgrid lines above hold the same
* effective values.  `yesno draw_minor_grid' is transcribed from Stata's
* file but, like draw_major_grid, no style file reads it.
yesno draw_minor_hgrid  no
yesno draw_minor_vgrid  no
yesno draw_minor_grid   no

* Text colours, one per textbox style: style/textboxstyle-<name>.style
* reads `color <name>'.  title() is heading (above), subtitle()
* subheading, caption() body, note() small_body, legend keys key_label,
* axis titles axis_title, tick labels tick_label.  `color text' and
* `color foreground' are the scheme's general text and foreground
* colours.
color subheading  #000000
color body        #000000
color small_body  #000000
color text        #000000
color axis_title  #000000
color tick_label  #000000
color key_label   #000000
color foreground  #000000

* Text sizes, one per textbox style (Stata gsize style names; the
* fixture's _meta.units gives each name's relative value and its points
* at Stata's 4-inch reference).  note() is drawn with small_body: the
* `gsize note' line above is not the key Stata's note textbox reads.
gsize subheading  medium
gsize body        medsmall
gsize small_body  small
gsize key_label   medsmall

* Minor grid and axis line widths (Stata linewidth style names).
linewidth minor_grid  thin
linewidth axisline    thin

* Legend rows; 0 means not fixed, so the column count above decides.
numstyle legend_rows  0

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* HONEST GAP (project rule 2) -- the p#lineplot keys
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Stata does not read a plain `twoway line` plot's line from the
* `p#line` scheme keys.  It reaches the `p#lineplot` keys through two
* style files:
*   style/seriesstyle-p#line.style:5   set line    scheme p#line
*   style/linestyle-p#line.style:4-7   set width   scheme p#lineplot
*                                      set color   scheme p#lineplot
*                                      set pattern scheme p#lineplot
* So `linepattern p#lineplot` is the dash pattern of `twoway line`, while
* the `linepattern p#line` block above is what style/linestyle-p#.style
* reads -- the connecting line of `twoway connected`.  Stata documents the
* three keys in scheme_line_plots.sthlp:29-34 as the colour, thickness and
* pattern "for #th line plot".
*
* NO `color p#lineplot` OR `linepattern p#lineplot` DIRECTIVE IS WRITTEN IN
* THIS FILE, because stgcolor's #include chain (s2color -> stcolor ->
* stgcolor) declares none at any level. Of all the scheme files Stata ships,
* only scheme-s2mono.scheme and scheme-s2gmanual.scheme contain a `lineplot`
* directive at all; the three occurrences in scheme-s2color.scheme (lines
* 457, 604 and 1250) are commented-out examples, not settings. There is
* therefore nothing to translate here, and a value written in would be a
* fabrication.
*
* Recorded for the reader, NOT as directives, because these are values Stata
* falls back to rather than values stgcolor writes
* (scheme_line_plots.sthlp:52-56: "If a scheme does not contain some of the
* line-plot specific entries above, then the look of those elements of line
* plots will be determined by default entries that are shared among all
* plottypes"; the shared entries are `color p#` and `linepattern p`, per
* scheme_shared_plots.sthlp:20-31):
*   linepattern p#lineplot -> linepattern p = solid
*       (/Applications/StataNow/ado/base/s/scheme-s2color.scheme:599)
*   color       p#lineplot -> color p#, i.e. the `color p1`..`color p15`
*       values already written above
*       (/Applications/StataNow/ado/base/s/scheme-stcolor.scheme:47-61)
